## Statement

Let $A=\mathrm{Cl}_{p,q}$ be a positive-dimensional standard real Clifford algebra with the canonical grading from [[lem-clifford-quotient-has-canonical-z2-grading]], and write $A^0$ for its even subalgebra. If $p>0$ then $A^0\cong\mathrm{Cl}_{q,p-1}$, and if $p=0$ then $A^0\cong\mathrm{Cl}_{0,q-1}$. For the standard complex Clifford algebra in dimension $n>0$, $A^0\cong\mathrm{Cl}_{0,n-1}(\mathbb C)$. Here an irreducible graded module means a nonzero graded module with no proper nonzero graded submodule ([[def-clifford-graded-algebra-module-and-tensor-product]]). Its isomorphism class, with parity-preserving maps, is exactly the class of a simple left $A^0$-module under the induction correspondence of [[thm-graded-module-classification-from-even-subalgebra]].

Consequently, if the applicable even-algebra entry in [[thm-real-complex-clifford-algebra-classification]] is $M_m(D)$, there is one irreducible graded module with even part the column module $D^m$; if it is $M_m(D)\oplus M_m(D)$, there are two, one supported on each factor. Their coefficient type is $D\in\{\mathbb R,\mathbb C,\mathbb H\}$. In complex scalars the type is always $\mathbb C$. At dimension zero, the algebra is purely even and has two irreducible graded modules, concentrated in parity $0$ and parity $1$, respectively.

For standard complex $\mathrm{Cl}_n(\mathbb C)$ with $n=2m>0$, the normalized volume element $\omega=i^m e_1\cdots e_n$ has square $1$, lies in the even subalgebra, and anticommutes with every odd generator. The two graded-module classes are the two choices of which $\omega$-eigenspace is placed in parity $0$; these are the two chiral parity conventions. For $n=2m+1$, the same formula gives an odd central element with square $1$; its two central idempotents are exchanged by the grading, so the two ungraded simple factors pair into the unique graded-simple class.

## Facts & Assumptions

**Given:** A standard nondegenerate real or complex Clifford algebra, its canonical $\mathbb Z/2$ grading, and the coefficient-algebra notation of [[def-real-quaternion-matrix-algebra-notation]].

[F1] Every graded-simple module over a graded algebra with an invertible odd element corresponds to a simple module over its even subalgebra ([[thm-graded-module-classification-from-even-subalgebra]]).

[F2] The even subalgebra has the stated lower-dimensional Clifford signature ([[lem-clifford-even-subalgebra-signature-identification]]).

[F3] The ungraded real and complex Clifford algebras have the matrix-algebra and split-component types in the classification table ([[thm-real-complex-clifford-algebra-classification]]).

[F4] For a finite product of matrix algebras over division rings, all simple modules are the column modules, one per factor ([[thm-simple-modules-over-semisimple-rings]]).

[F5] $\mathbb R$ and $\mathbb C$ are fields, and $\mathbb H$ is a division algebra; quaternion conjugation identifies $\mathbb H^{\mathrm{op}}$ with $\mathbb H$ ([[thm-reals-ordered-field]], [[thm-complex-numbers-form-a-field]], [[lem-real-quaternions-form-a-division-algebra]]).

[F6] The ordered square-free Clifford monomials form a basis, in particular the top monomial in positive dimension is nonzero and is linearly independent of $1$ ([[thm-clifford-pbw-basis-and-associated-graded]]).

[F7] Orthogonal Clifford generators anticommute ([[lem-clifford-polarized-relations]]).

[F8] The Clifford algebra is the quotient generated by canonical vectors satisfying $\bar v^{\,2}=Q(v)1$ ([[def-clifford-algebra-of-a-quadratic-space]]).

[F9] The canonical quotient grading makes each Clifford generator odd ([[lem-clifford-quotient-has-canonical-z2-grading]]).

[F10] The Clifford algebra of the zero vector space over its base field is that field ([[lem-clifford-zero-dimensional-algebra-is-base-field]]).

## Proof

**Proof technique:** even-subalgebra reduction.

1.1 In the quotient [F8], let $n=p+q>0$ in the real case, or $n>0$ in the complex case. A standard nondegenerate form has a generator $u$ with $u^2=1$ or $u^2=-1$, so $u$ is odd by [F9] and invertible. By [F1], irreducible graded $A$-modules correspond bijectively to simple $A^0$-modules; [F2] identifies $A^0$ with the lower-dimensional Clifford algebra stated in the theorem. [given, F1, F2, F8, F9, construct]

1.2 In standard complex dimension $n$, let $e_1,\ldots,e_n$ be the standard generators, so $e_j^2=1$ by [F8], and set $\Omega=e_1\cdots e_n$ and $\omega=i^{\lfloor n/2\rfloor}\Omega$. Moving $e_j$ past the other $n-1$ orthogonal generators gives $\Omega e_j=(-1)^{n-1}e_j\Omega$ by [F7], while pairing the generators in $\Omega^2$ gives $\Omega^2=(-1)^{n(n-1)/2}\prod_{j=1}^n e_j^2=(-1)^{n(n-1)/2}$ by [F7] and [F8]. For $n=2m$ and for $n=2m+1$, the scalar factor has square $(-1)^m$, so $\omega^2=1$ in both cases. Its parity is $n\bmod2$ by [F9]; hence for even $n$ it is even and anticommutes with odd generators, while for odd $n$ it is odd and commutes with every generator. [given, F7, F8, F9, algebra]

2.1 By [F3], the even algebra $A^0$ is either a single matrix algebra $M_m(D)$ or a direct sum of two such algebras, with $D$ one of $\mathbb R,\mathbb C,\mathbb H$ in the real case and $D=\mathbb C$ in the complex case. Each $D$ is a division ring by [F5], so [F4] gives one simple column module $D^m$ in the single-factor case and exactly two simple column modules, each supported on one factor, in the split case. [F3, F4, F5, step 1.1, algebra]

3.1 For the column module $D^m$ (column vectors with left matrix action), every $M_m(D)$-linear endomorphism is right multiplication by a unique $d\in D$: commuting with $E_{11}$ forces the image of the first standard column to be $e_1d$, and commuting with $E_{i1}(a)$ gives the image $e_iad$ of every column $e_i a$. Conversely right multiplication commutes with the left matrix action. Composition reverses the order of the right multipliers, so $\operatorname{End}_{M_m(D)}(D^m)\cong D^{\mathrm{op}}$. For $D=\mathbb R,\mathbb C$ this is $D$, and for $D=\mathbb H$ quaternion conjugation gives $D^{\mathrm{op}}\cong D$ by [F5]. [given, F5, step 2.1, algebra]

4.1 Combining steps 1.1, 2.1, and 3.1 yields the asserted graded-module counts and coefficient types, including both modules when the even algebra splits. If $n=0$, [F10] gives $A=F$ for $F=\mathbb R$ or $\mathbb C$, and there is no odd component. Each parity component of a graded module is then an $A$-submodule, so a graded-simple module must be nonzero in exactly one parity. Any nonzero vector in that component generates a nonzero submodule $Fv$, which by simplicity is the entire component; the map $F\to Fv$, $c\mapsto cv$, is injective and surjective, so the module is one-dimensional over $F$. The two parity placements are nonisomorphic as even graded modules. [given, F1, F2, F4, F5, F10, step 1.1, step 2.1, step 3.1, algebra]

5.1 If $n=2m>0$, [F2] and [F3] identify the even algebra with a split sum of two complex matrix algebras. The element $\omega$ is central in the even algebra; by [F6] it is not $1$ or $-1$, so $(1+\omega)/2$ and $(1-\omega)/2$ are the two nonzero factor idempotents. The simple degree-zero modules therefore have $\omega$-eigenvalue $+1$ or $-1$, and multiplication by any odd invertible generator switches that eigenvalue in degree $1$; this is precisely the two chiral parity choices. If $n=2m+1$, [F2] and [F3] give a simple even algebra and hence one graded-simple class by step 1.1. In the full algebra $\omega$ is odd central and not $\pm1$ by [F6], so its idempotents $(1\pm\omega)/2$ are nonzero and split the two ungraded matrix factors; the parity operator $P(x^0+x^1)=x^0-x^1$ satisfies $P a P^{-1}=(-1)^{|a|}a$ and exchanges these idempotents. Thus a nonzero graded module contains both ungraded factors, as stated. [F1, F2, F3, F6, step 1.1, step 1.2, algebra] ∎
